From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Onyx contributors <onyx@localhost>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 18:01:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 05/24] Onyx: the mutator finishes the collections before it
 releases heap access

What: heap/Heap.h, heap/Heap.cpp: for OS(ONYX), Heap::releaseAccess() first calls
finishCollectionsBeforeReleasingAccess(): while the collector has an outstanding request, the
mutator waits for all collections (waitForAllCollections(): it runs the phases itself when it
has the conn, and is stopped at a safepoint with its CurrentThreadState otherwise). Nothing is
done when the heap is not safe to collect, is shutting down, or the mutator is itself
collecting.

Why: a collection that goes on after the mutator released heap access (the VM's lock dropped:
the thread is back in its run loop) is conducted by the collector thread, which has to suspend
the mutator's thread to scan its stack and registers (MachineThreads::
tryCopyOtherThreadStacks). Onyx has no asynchronous signals and cannot suspend a thread
(Thread::suspend() is fatal there): a program that allocated, then waited in its run loop
(setTimeout in the jsc shell; any page in a browser) was ended by that assertion as soon as a
collection was still running or pending when it went idle. With this, a thread's stack is only
ever scanned by the thread itself. Collections are requested by the thread that holds the VM's
lock, so none starts while the mutator has no access. The cost: the end of a collection is a
pause on the mutator when it goes idle, instead of running beside it; marking is still
concurrent and parallel while the mutator runs. A kernel call to suspend a thread and read its
registers would lift this (and allow a VM shared between threads).
---
 Source/JavaScriptCore/heap/Heap.cpp | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 Source/JavaScriptCore/heap/Heap.h   |  6 ++++++
 2 files changed, 25 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Source/JavaScriptCore/heap/Heap.cpp b/Source/JavaScriptCore/heap/Heap.cpp
index 9e0f4956..9ffda470 100644
--- a/Source/JavaScriptCore/heap/Heap.cpp
+++ b/Source/JavaScriptCore/heap/Heap.cpp
@@ -1552,6 +1552,25 @@ void Heap::acquireAccessSlow()
     }
 }
 
+#if OS(ONYX)
+// A collection that goes on after the mutator released heap access runs in the collector thread,
+// which then has to suspend the mutator's thread to scan its stack and registers
+// (MachineThreads::tryCopyOtherThreadStacks). Onyx cannot suspend a thread (no asynchronous
+// signals: Thread::suspend() is fatal there). So the mutator never leaves a collection behind:
+// it waits for the requested collections (running their phases itself when it has the conn,
+// its own stack scanned through its CurrentThreadState) before it gives up heap access. No
+// collection starts while it has no access: requests come from the thread holding the VM's lock.
+void Heap::finishCollectionsBeforeReleasingAccess()
+{
+    if (!m_isSafeToCollect || m_isShuttingDown || mutatorState() != MutatorState::Running)
+        return;
+    if (!(m_worldState.load() & hasAccessBit))
+        return;
+    while (m_collector->hasOutstandingRequest())
+        waitForAllCollections();
+}
+#endif
+
 void Heap::releaseAccessSlow()
 {
     for (;;) {
diff --git a/Source/JavaScriptCore/heap/Heap.h b/Source/JavaScriptCore/heap/Heap.h
index 41046409..ef6ab109 100644
--- a/Source/JavaScriptCore/heap/Heap.h
+++ b/Source/JavaScriptCore/heap/Heap.h
@@ -553,6 +553,9 @@ public:
     // on the ParkingLot.
     void releaseAccess()
     {
+#if OS(ONYX)
+        finishCollectionsBeforeReleasingAccess();
+#endif
         if (m_worldState.compareExchangeWeak(hasAccessBit, 0))
             return;
         releaseAccessSlow();
@@ -705,6 +708,9 @@ private:
     
     JS_EXPORT_PRIVATE void acquireAccessSlow();
     JS_EXPORT_PRIVATE void releaseAccessSlow();
+#if OS(ONYX)
+    JS_EXPORT_PRIVATE void finishCollectionsBeforeReleasingAccess();
+#endif
     
     bool handleNeedCollectionEpilogue(unsigned);
     void handleNeedCollectionEpilogue();
